BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alliance-gender-equality-strengthening-movements-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alliance for Gender Equality in Europe: Strengthening Mov
 ements for Gender Equality (2026 call)
DESCRIPTION:NOTE: this is a gender-equality movement-building fund rather t
 han an arts or technology programme\, and it is coalition-only (no individ
 ual applicants). Relevance to creative or tech practice runs through the n
 arrative-change and digital-storytelling strand of the call. The Alliance 
 for Gender Equality in Europe\, a philanthropic initiative created in 2021
  and hosted by the Network of European Foundations\, is launching an open 
 call under its forthcoming Strengthening Movements Fund. The Alliance is c
 omposed of the Bodossaki Foundation\, Fondation BNP Paribas\, Fondation CH
 ANEL\, Fondazione con il Sud\, Fondation de France\, Fondation RAJA-Daniè
 le Marcovici\, King Baudouin Foundation and L'Oréal Fund for Women\; this
  particular fund is backed by all of those except Fondation BNP Paribas. O
 BJECTIVE: to foster collaboration and solidarity within European intersect
 ional and grassroots movements so that they can collectively advance gende
 r equality in Europe in the long term. The call follows a 2025 Alliance-co
 mmissioned mapping of European gender-equality movements (30 in-depth inte
 rviews plus contextual research) which found movements under-resourced bec
 ause philanthropic funding frameworks are misaligned with their needs\; th
 e fund is designed to replace short-term reactive funding with proactive l
 ong-term support. OUTCOMES SOUGHT: community organising reinforced at loca
 l or national level\; pan-European feminist collaborations and solidarity 
 strengthened\; alliances between gender equality and other social justice 
 movements expanded\; pro-gender-equality narratives and voices amplified i
 n local\, national\, regional or digital spaces\; policy debates informed 
 by feminist and gender equality movements. ELIGIBLE ACTIVITIES INCLUDE (no
 n-exhaustive): national\, regional or pan-European convenings and collabor
 ative projects where groups co-create joint strategies\; joint campaigns b
 etween feminist movements and allied movements (climate justice\, anti-rac
 ism\, LGBTQI+\, disability rights)\; narrative-change training for grassro
 ots groups covering framing\, messaging\, digital tools and digital storyt
 elling\; cross-border coalitions advocating EU institutions or national go
 vernments\; facilitated virtual or in-person convenings to work through in
 ternal tensions and build trust\; translating academic research on anti-ge
 nder efforts into movement strategies. ELIGIBILITY: collective proposals w
 ith at least 3 organisations per coalition (no maximum)\; legally register
 ed non-profits (NGOs\, civil society organisations\, social enterprises\, 
 national or regional funds)\; based and operating in the target countries\
 ; small and medium grassroots organisations\, defined by annual budget und
 er EUR 450\,000 (Austria\, Belgium\, Denmark\, Finland\, France\, Germany\
 , Ireland\, Luxembourg\, Netherlands\, Sweden\, UK) or under EUR 300\,000 
 (Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cypru
 s\, Czechia\, Estonia\, Greece\, Hungary\, Italy\, Kosovo\, Latvia\, Lithu
 ania\, Malta\, Montenegro\, North Macedonia\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\
 , Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Serbia). Intermediary organisations and ne
 tworks are exempt from the budget thresholds but must apply in coalition w
 ith grassroots organisations\, with the budget fairly distributed among al
 l partners. Coalitions must be community-led (majority of leadership roles
  held by members of the community supported\, and/or participatory approac
 hes)\, work with marginalised women\, transgender\, non-binary and gender 
 non-conforming people\, use gender transformative approaches\, and hold an
  intersectional perspective. Proposals must be completed in English. Each 
 organisation may submit only one application\, as lead or as partner. One 
 organisation per coalition acts as lead\, is the primary contact and is le
 gally responsible for the grant\; leads are invited to networking\, peer-l
 earning and capacity-strengthening activities. SCORING (100 points): relev
 ance to the call objective 20\; demonstrated track record in strengthening
  movements 20 (learnings valued as well as achievements)\; strong impact 2
 5 (must contribute to at least one of the five outcomes\, with clear measu
 rement)\; ability to work collaboratively and inclusively with partners 20
  (including fair budget repartition)\; quality and feasibility 15. PROCESS
 : two stages. Stage 1 opened 29 June 2026 and closes 7 August 2026 at 23:5
 9 CEST\, submitted in English via the online platform (a PDF of the form i
 s downloadable for reference only). Pre-selection results by email 14 Sept
 ember 2026\; stage 2 runs 16 September to 23 October 2026 and includes due
 -diligence contact from Alliance staff\; final results by email in Decembe
 r 2026\; grants start January 2027. The Alliance is piloting a participato
 ry selection process for this call\, with grant-making decisions made join
 tly by a committee of funders and non-funders holding equal decision-makin
 g power. Q&A webinars: 6 July 2026 at 14:00 CEST and 15 July 2026 at 10:00
  CEST (registration in advance\; both have passed). An updated FAQ is publ
 ished on the call page.\n\nOrganisation: Alliance for Gender Equality in E
 urope (hosted by the Network of European Foundations)\nLocation: European 
 Union\, United Kingdom\, Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina\, Kosovo\, Monte
 negro\, North Macedonia and Serbia. All coalition partners must be based a
 nd operating in these countries.\nAward: EUR 2\,700\,000 total across the 
 call. Between EUR 225\,000 and EUR 450\,000 per grant over 3 years (EUR 75
 \,000 to EUR 150\,000 per year). Project support including salary and over
 head costs\; overheads capped at 20 percent. 6 to 12 coalitions expected t
 o be funded. Free to apply.\nDuration: 3 years\, January 2027 to December 
 2029. Renewal possible at the end of the cycle subject to budget.\n\nMore:
  https://alliance-genderequality.org/call-for-proposals-strengthening-move
 ments-for-gender-equality/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://alliance-genderequality.org/call-for-proposals-strengthening-mo
 vements-for-gender-equality/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:European Union\, United Kingdom\, Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina
 \, Kosovo\, Montenegro\, North Macedonia and Serbia. All coalition partner
 s must be based and operating in these countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:red-quincho-beca-constelaciones-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
SUMMARY:Deadline: Red Quincho: Beca Constelaciones 2026 (4th Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Beca Constelaciones is Red Quincho's mobility and production gr
 ant for artists\, curators and researchers doing territory-based work\, no
 w in its fourth edition. Each recipient spends one month in residence\, sp
 lit into two 15-day placements at different member spaces across Argentina
 \, pairing an urban and a rural context. The grant covers mobility\, livin
 g costs and production\, with lodging provided. Backed by Fundacion Willia
 ms\, CCEBA and Fundacion Santander through the CABA Mecenazgo scheme. ELIG
 IBILITY IS RESTRICTED: of the 15 grants\, 13 are for people resident in Ar
 gentina and 2 are for people currently living in Spain. Artists\, curators
  and researchers apply through separate forms\; Spanish-based candidates f
 ace an additional jury evaluation. Deadline 9 August 2026 at 23:59 Argenti
 na time.\n\nOrganisation: Red Quincho\nLocation: Rotating urban and rural 
 Red Quincho spaces across Argentina\; residency runs September to December
  2026.\nAward: ARS 1\,000\,000 (around USD 690) per grant toward mobility\
 , living costs and production\, plus one month of lodging. 15 grants avail
 able. No application fee.\nDuration: One-month residency split into two 15
 -day placements at different spaces.\n\nMore: https://redquincho.ar/beca-c
 onstelaciones-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://redquincho.ar/beca-constelaciones-2026/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Rotating urban and rural Red Quincho spaces across Argentina\; res
 idency runs September to December 2026.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kone-foundation-annual-grant-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kone Foundation: Annual Grant Call (2026)
DESCRIPTION:Kone Foundation is Finland's major private funder for academic 
 research in the humanities\, social sciences and environmental sciences\, 
 for professional artistic work across all disciplines\, and specifically f
 or multidisciplinary projects combining scholarly and artistic approaches\
 , which is where digital culture\, media arts and artistic research propos
 als land. Individuals\, working groups and legal entities with a Finnish b
 usiness ID may apply\, and artistic groups and organisations can receive u
 p to EUR 250\,000. The Finland connection is the binding constraint: a non
 -Finnish applicant needs a Finnish institutional affiliation\, Finland res
 idence\, or the work must happen in Finland\, though international collabo
 rators can join a group freely once one member is a Finnish citizen or Fin
 land-based. Psychology\, theology\, law\, business studies without an inte
 rdisciplinary element\, and medical research are not funded\, nor are mast
 er's studies\, continuing education\, building renovation\, standalone equ
 ipment purchases or hobby activity. The annual call runs 1 to 15 September
  each year\, closing 15 September 2026 at 16:00 EEST\, with decisions anno
 unced in November and December. TIMING: funding is never retroactive and p
 rojects must begin in January or later\, so a September 2026 application f
 unds work starting January 2027 at the earliest. FLAG: Kone frames its fun
 ding around artistic work and research projects rather than festival or ev
 ent hosting as such\, so an event-led application needs to be framed as an
  artistic or research project.\n\nOrganisation: Kone Foundation / Koneen S
 aatio (Finland)\nLocation: Finland. Work may be carried out abroad by Finn
 ish citizens. Projects must have a connection to Finland.\nAward: Artistic
  groups and organisations up to EUR 250\,000\; research groups typically E
 UR 200\,000 to 500\,000. Minimum project duration six months for individua
 l applicants and one month for groups\; maximum four consecutive years for
  the same project. No co-financing requirement stated. No application fee.
  No retroactive funding.\nDuration: One month to four years\; projects mus
 t start in January or later.\n\nMore: https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants/gr
 antapplicants/criteria-for-applying/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants/grantapplicants/criteria-for-applying
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Finland. Work may be carried out abroad by Finnish citizens. Proje
 cts must have a connection to Finland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uk-irl-culture-connects-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: British Council Ireland: UK-IRL Culture Connects Grant 20
 26/27
DESCRIPTION:The UK-IRL Culture Connects Grant 2026/27 is a British Council 
 Ireland initiative delivered in association with Arts Council Ireland\, su
 pporting the early stages of artistic collaboration between Ireland and th
 e four UK nations through in-person exchange. It prioritises creative deve
 lopment over final outputs: research and development of new ideas\, mentor
 ship\, exploring collaborative pathways with producers or partners\, exper
 imenting with new technologies or practices\, and developing initiatives t
 hat support creative exchange. WHO: applications are made by individual ar
 tists or creative practitioners working as a bilateral partnership of one 
 individual based in Ireland and one based in England\, Northern Ireland\, 
 Scotland or Wales\; applicants must be resident in Ireland or the UK for t
 he project duration\, have a proven professional track record\, be at an e
 arly or developing career stage\, and not be in full-time education. One a
 pplication per collaboration. FUNDING: up to EUR 3\,000 covering artist an
 d collaborator fees\, travel and accommodation\, studio hire\, materials\,
  and access costs. Activities must involve in-person collaboration (fully 
 remote projects are not eligible) and take place between October 2026 and 
 31 March 2027. Apply via the British Council GoodGrants platform\; an info
 rmation webinar recording and overview document are on the programme page.
  DEADLINE: 31 July 2026.\n\nOrganisation: British Council Ireland / Arts C
 ouncil Ireland\nLocation: Ireland and the UK. For bilateral partnerships o
 f one individual based in Ireland and one based in England\, Northern Irel
 and\, Scotland or Wales\; in-person collaboration required.\nAward: Up to 
 EUR 3\,000 per collaboration (smaller amounts also eligible)\, covering ar
 tist fees (including your own time)\, collaborator fees\, travel and accom
 modation\, studio or space hire\, materials\, and access costs including d
 isability support. No application fee.\nDuration: Activity between October
  2026 and March 2027\; must be completed by 31 March 2027\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.britishcouncil.ie/programmes/arts/uk-irl-culture-connects-grant-20262
 7?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.britishcouncil.ie/programmes/arts/uk-irl-culture-connects-g
 rant-202627?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ireland and the UK. For bilateral partnerships of one individual b
 ased in Ireland and one based in England\, Northern Ireland\, Scotland or 
 Wales\; in-person collaboration required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:simons-foundation-open-interval-turbulence-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Interval: An Artist-Scientist Exploration of Turbule
 nce
DESCRIPTION:Open Interval is a Simons Foundation program that supports trio
 s consisting of an artist\, a scientist\, and a representative from a 501(
 c)(3) arts organization to collaborate around a shared scientific theme. T
 he current cycle centers on the theme of turbulence\, a phenomenon at the 
 edge of chaos and beauty. Awarded trios receive USD 30\,000 to support a s
 ustained period of thought partnership and exploration rather than the pro
 duction of finished works or exhibitions. The program runs from April to D
 ecember 2027 with a touchstone meeting in May 2027 and possible cohort con
 venings. Trio members must be regionally co-located anywhere in the United
  States so they can collaborate in person. The scientist should hold a Ph.
 D. and be conducting basic research\, with priority given to fields such a
 s astrophysics\, neuroscience\, quantum physics\, biology\, or mathematics
 . The application portal opens September 9\, 2026\, and all three trio mem
 bers must submit for the application to be complete.\n\nOrganisation: Simo
 ns Foundation\nLocation: United States (trio must be regionally co-located
 )\nAward: USD 30\,000 per awarded trio\nDuration: April to December 2027 (
 several months of collaboration)\n\nMore: https://www.simonsfoundation.org
 /2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-an-artist-scientist-exploration-of-t
 urbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-a
 n-artist-scientist-exploration-of-turbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (trio must be regionally co-located)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:british-council-connections-through-culture-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: Connections Through Culture Grants 2026 (British Council)
DESCRIPTION:Connections Through Culture (CTC) grants are designed to enable
  meaningful partnerships\, cross-cultural exchange and long-term collabora
 tion between the UK and participating countries. In 2026\, CTC incorporate
 s the grants programmes formerly known as Biennials Connect (Visual Arts) 
 and Architecture\, Design and Fashion Biennials and Festivals Grants\, and
  serves as the British Council's major Arts grants programme for 2026 (in 
 place of International Collaboration Grants). It offers grants of between 
 GBP 5\,000 and GBP 15\,000 to support creative practitioners\, cultural pr
 ofessionals\, artists\, organisations\, festivals\, biennials\, hubs and c
 ollectives to develop and deliver collaborative projects across a wide ran
 ge of themes and art forms\, including applied arts\, architecture\, curat
 ing\, dance\, design\, digital\, drawing\, education\, fashion\, film\, in
 stallation\, interdisciplinary work\, painting\, performance\, photography
 \, printmaking\, public art\, research\, sculpture\, social practice\, sou
 nd art\, textiles\, video\, visual arts and writing. ELIGIBILITY: applicat
 ions must involve collaboration between at least one UK-based partner and 
 at least one partner based in an eligible participating country\; each par
 tner must be legally based in the country they are applying from at the ti
 me of application and throughout the project activity period. PARTICIPATIN
 G COUNTRIES: Albania\, Argentina\, Australia\, Bangladesh\, Bosnia and Her
 zegovina\, Colombia\, Mainland China\, Egypt\, Indonesia\, Kazakhstan\, Ke
 nya\, Kosovo\, Lebanon\, Malaysia\, Mexico\, Montenegro\, New Zealand\, Ni
 geria\, North Macedonia\, Pakistan\, Palestine\, Peru\, Philippines\, Saud
 i Arabia\, Serbia\, South Africa\, Tanzania\, Thailand\, Tunisia\, Turkey\
 , United Arab Emirates\, UK\, Ukraine\, Uzbekistan\, Venezuela\, and Vietn
 am. Eligible applicants include artists and creative practitioners\, arts 
 and cultural organisations\, festivals and biennials\, cultural profession
 als\, creative hubs/networks/collectives\, and organisations working acros
 s arts\, culture and the creative sectors. Note: applications involving pr
 ojects organised by or taking place as part of Visual Arts\, Architecture\
 , Design\, Fashion and Craft festivals should be made by the festival or b
 iennial organisation rather than by individual artists\, designers or prac
 titioners. No application fee. Deadline 12 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: B
 ritish Council\nLocation: International collaboration grant. Each applicat
 ion must involve at least one UK-based partner and at least one partner ba
 sed in an eligible participating country\; each partner must be legally ba
 sed in the country they apply from throughout the project.\nAward: Grants 
 of between GBP 5\,000 and GBP 15\,000. Free to apply.\nDuration: Project-b
 ased\; develop and deliver collaborative projects across a range of themes
  and art forms.\n\nMore: https://arts.britishcouncil.org/connections-throu
 gh-culture?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.britishcouncil.org/connections-through-culture?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:International collaboration grant. Each application must involve a
 t least one UK-based partner and at least one partner based in an eligible
  participating country\; each partner must be legally based in the country
  they apply from throughout the project.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:siap-project-funding-individuals-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: SIAP: Project Funding for Individual Artists 2026 (Arts C
 ouncil NI)
DESCRIPTION:Project Funding for Individuals\, part of Arts Council NI's Nat
 ional Lottery Support for Individual Artists Programme (SIAP)\, funds proj
 ect-based creative activities across all artistic disciplines. Eligible ac
 tivities include creating new work\, research and development\, residencie
 s\, mentoring\, professional development\, and time dedicated to specific 
 creative outcomes. ELIGIBLE COSTS: art materials\, artist fees/time\, resi
 dency costs\, travel (mileage at GBP 0.257/mile)\, venue hire\, technical 
 equipment (max 50% of request value)\, and IT equipment (laptops/PCs up to
  GBP 1\,200\; tablets up to GBP 500). ELIGIBILITY: artists of all discipli
 nes and career stages\, including emerging artists (less than 5 years prof
 essional experience)\, and in some cases technical staff/administrators\; 
 students and academics at third-level institutions may apply with departme
 ntal approval showing the costs are not covered by their employer or acade
 mic work. AMOUNT: GBP 62 to GBP 15\,000. No application fee. Deadline: Thu
 rsday 30 July 2026\, 12:00 noon (online submission required).\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Arts Council of Northern Ireland\nLocation: Northern Ireland\, United
  Kingdom. Applicants must be based in Northern Ireland.\nAward: GBP 62 to 
 GBP 15\,000. Funded through the National Lottery Support for Individual Ar
 tists Programme (SIAP).\nDuration: Project-based activity\; duration not s
 pecified.\n\nMore: https://artscouncil-ni.org/funding-for-individuals/proj
 ect-funding-for-individuals?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://artscouncil-ni.org/funding-for-individuals/project-funding-for-
 individuals?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Northern Ireland\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in Nor
 thern Ireland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:siap-artists-international-development-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: SIAP: Artists' International Development Fund 2026 (Arts 
 Council NI)
DESCRIPTION:The Artists' International Development Fund (AIDF)\, part of Ar
 ts Council NI's National Lottery Support for Individual Artists Programme 
 (SIAP)\, funds early-stage international development opportunities for ind
 ividual\, freelance and self-employed artists to build networks abroad. EL
 IGIBLE COSTS: art materials\, travel\, residencies (up to 4 weeks)\, techn
 ical fees\, venue hire\, and equipment\, with IT devices capped at GBP 1\,
 200 for laptops/PCs and GBP 500 for tablets. ELIGIBILITY: artists across a
 ll disciplines\, particularly emerging artists (under 5 years professional
  experience)\; academics and students at third-level institutions may appl
 y if the project differs from their employment/studies. AIDF applicants mu
 st have received recognition for their work in Northern Ireland\, lack ext
 ensive international experience\, and demonstrate interest in their work f
 rom an overseas partner/host. AMOUNT: GBP 62 to GBP 15\,000. No applicatio
 n fee. Deadline: Thursday 30 July 2026\, 12:00 noon.\n\nOrganisation: Arts
  Council of Northern Ireland\nLocation: Northern Ireland\, United Kingdom.
  Applicants must be based in Northern Ireland\; international development 
 takes place abroad with overseas partners/hosts.\nAward: GBP 62 to GBP 15\
 ,000. Funded through the National Lottery Support for Individual Artists P
 rogramme (SIAP).\nDuration: Early-stage international development opportun
 ities\; residencies supported for a maximum of 4 weeks.\n\nMore: https://a
 rtscouncil-ni.org/funding-for-individuals/artists-international-developmen
 t-fund-individuals?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://artscouncil-ni.org/funding-for-individuals/artists-internationa
 l-development-fund-individuals?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Northern Ireland\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in Nor
 thern Ireland\; international development takes place abroad with overseas
  partners/hosts.
